# Denylist for scripts/dev/publishability-test.sh — COPY to .publish-denylist
# and fill in the REAL values. The real file is gitignored on purpose: it
# describes the estate, so committing it would publish exactly what the gate
# exists to keep out. See the script header.
#
# Format: <class-name><TAB><extended-regex>
#
# host-ips	203\.0\.113\.(7|8|9)
# control-plane-ip	198\.51\.100\.4
# secret-store-ip	192\.0\.2\.55
# internal-domain	[a-z0-9-]+\.internal\.example
# host-names	myorg-node-[0-9]+
# overlay-net	100\.64\.[0-9]+\.
# provider	(acme ?cloud|somedatacentre)
#
# Certificate-borne identifiers are scanned as DECODED HEX, so write them
# in hex (uppercase, no separators). An AMD VCEK carries the 64-byte
# CHIP_ID of a physical host in an X.509 extension: a text search over the
# PEM returns nothing, because the bytes are base64 inside DER. That is not
# hypothetical — it is how a real chip id survived a scrub that renamed the
# file it lived in.
#
# chip-ids	DEADBEEF0123456789ABCDEF0123456789ABCDEF
#
# The class NAMES are pinned in scripts/dev/publishability-test.sh
# (`EXPECTED_CLASSES`). A list missing one of them is a hard failure, not a
# shorter scan — because the list lives outside the repo, in two copies (a
# local file and a CI secret), and two copies drift. Retiring a class is
# allowed; doing it silently is not.
#
# On-chain identity is a fingerprint even though it is not a secret. An SS58
# address is public by construction, but naming YOUR administrative authority
# account in a published repo tells a reader which key to target without them
# having to know where to look.
#
# chain-accounts	5FAKEfakeFAKEfakeFAKEfakeFAKEfakeFAKEfakeFAKEfake
#
# Your organisation's role addresses. Not secrets, but identities: an ACME
# contact hardcoded in a chart means whoever deploys it registers a
# certificate account under YOUR address and receives YOUR expiry mail.
#
# org-emails	(ops|admin|security)@example\.invalid
#
# Cover the name a machine gives ITSELF, not only the name your inventory
# gives it. Ours differ: ansible and k8s say `hippius-cc-miner-3`, the box's
# own hostname is `confidential-compute-miner-3`. The second is what lands in
# kernel logs, serial captures and pasted terminal output — the things that
# actually get committed by accident — and a class written from the inventory
# alone is blind to all of it.
#
# host-names	(inventory-name-[0-9]+|actual-hostname-[0-9]+)
#
# Do NOT put people's home addresses in here.
#
# Auditing our own mesh turned up three public IPs the host-ips class did not
# know: one was a server nobody had told the list about (added), and two were
# the ISP addresses of colleagues' laptops. Those are personal data and they
# change with a DHCP lease, so encoding them by value would mean storing
# personal data on disk and in a CI secret in order to avoid storing it —
# and the entry would be stale within weeks.
#
# What actually covers that risk is shape, not value: the likeliest way a
# personal address reaches the tree is a pasted terminal capture, and the
# root-level `/*.log` ignore blocks the usual vector. Sweep for bare public
# IPv4 periodically instead, and triage what it finds by hand — most hits
# will be legitimate third-party addresses (distro mirrors, RFC examples).
#
# The three classes below have no illustrative line above; every class the
# gate expects needs one, so they are here for completeness.
#
# cluster-net	10\.0\.0\.[0-9]+
# node-ids	(deadbeef0123|feedface4567)
